/*******************************************************************************
                    What Is parser Doing
*******************************************************************************/
    parser turns a rule's TERMS into a generated parse body, compiles it, and
    installs it -- replacing the interpreted term-walk for that rule and every
    LIST-BEARING sub-rule reachable from it. Data-bearing rules, bins and tokens get
    no body; see the refusal causes below.

        parser(SomeRule);

    Two phases, in this order, and the order is the whole design:

        walkRules(argument)      GENERATE. Emit a body for the root and for
                                 every rule under it. Installs nothing.
        compileRules(argument)   INSTALL. setParse classifies and binds the
                                 parse method; compile parses the emitted text
                                 into a BlocK that can actually run.

    Nothing is installed during the walk. setParse leaves an already-installed rule
    alone, so a premature raise of the flag silently ends the job -- that is what
    F-90 measured (fixIts F-90).

    WHAT generateParse EMITS. It walks the rule's LIST in order -- `iterate grup on
    argument` carries no filter and has to reach MEMBERS, or GrouP's body could not
    exist -- skipping noPrint entries, and writes a call per term:

        NamE = CodE { return first() && nameSet(); }
        GrouP = CodE { return NamE() || QuotE(); }

    The connective is chosen by hasTraitS: **&&** for a SEQUENCE, whose terms are
    ATTRIBUTES, and **||** for an ALTERNATION, whose terms are MEMBERS. hasTraitS
    counts non-noPrint ATTRIBUTES, so an alternation reads 0 and takes the || arm --
    it is not that GrouP lacks real terms, it is that its terms are members.
    noPrint is this tree's standing "artifact, not a term" mark, which is why CodE,
    builtinActoR and builtinParseR never appear in an emitted body even though they
    are attributes like any other.

    A rule carrying DATA rather than a list is refused: it is appended to the
    uncompiled field and generation returns. A rule with both data and a list is
    a contradiction and says so on stderr.

    WHERE THE BODY GOES -- and this is the part that changed on 2026-09-20.
    The generated parse is parked in a builtinParseR carrier, which is a noPrint
    attribute holding its own CodE, exactly mirroring builtinActoR:

        SomeRule            length 6
            <its terms>
            builtinActoR        noPrint            the ACTION, if it has one
                CodE                               the action body
            builtinParseR       noPrint            the generated PARSE
                CodE                               the emitted text
                BlocK                              once compiled

    ⚠ builtinActoR HAS TWO SHAPES AND THE DIAGRAM ABOVE IS ONLY ONE OF THEM. For a
    CODED rule it carries a CodE, as drawn. For an UNCODED rule -- the 86 grammar
    rules, which take setActions' dlsym arm -- it carries a BARE METHOD POINTER and no
    CodE at all: `builtinActoR  noPrint  no data`, no length. Measured on Search.
    So "has a builtinActoR" never means "has a body". Ask for the CodE, not the carrier
    -- which is exactly what actionHolder does.

    ⚠ AND THE LIVE `clear(CodE); CodE = codeBuffer;` IS NOT A COUNTEREXAMPLE. `CodE`
    is UNDECLARED in this file's define block, so it is an action LOCAL (bear-trap #39)
    -- the emitted text is written into that local, and what reaches the tree is
    `copyOf(CodE)` attached INTO the carrier. The local is named CodE so that the copy
    carries that tag, which is the same trick the old builtinParseR line used.

    THE RULE ITSELF CARRIES NO CodE. Certificate: dumpContents(Search) after
    parser(Search) reads length 6 -- search, followedBy, GrouP, SemI, builtinActoR,
    builtinParseR -- and no CodE. That is what retired ownActionAtEntry: the
    old code had to ask "does this rule already have an action in CodE, so that
    writing the parse there would delete it" -- and once the action moved to its
    own carrier that question has no instances. Two carriers, each with exactly
    one meaning, and nothing to contest.

    WHY TWO CARRIERS AND NOT ONE SLOT WITH A RULE. A rule can carry an action AND
    a generated parse at the same time. A single "where is the body" answer would
    have to mean two things at once, which is this project's most expensive
    recurring shape. So GroupItem carries two accessor trios --
    actionHolder/actionBody/actionBlocK and parseHolder/parseBody/parseBlocK --
    and every reader names the one it means. processCode takes the holder as a
    second argument for the same reason.

    WHO FIRES WHAT, afterwards:
        parseRule          fires builtinParseR's BlocK -- the parse
        fireLabelMethod    fires builtinActoR's method -- the action
    Two seats over two bodies with NO BODY IN COMMON. ⚠ They are not otherwise
    independent: they still share isCodeD on the rule, today's argument slot, and the
    LABEL -- and the label is CT-5, which is open.

